    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Mechanical models provide a quantitative link between displacement fields, which are directly related to seismic reflection images and interpreted structure contour maps, and the stress fields associated with thrust faulting and folding. In this study at Elk Hills Oil Field, which developed within a syn-depositional tectonic environment, we assess the extent to which elastic models of displacement fields correlate with kinematic data. A good correlation would indicate that the associated stress fields may be helpful in the characterization of the secondary, subseismic deformation (joints, small faults, and sheared joints). Based on stratigraphic constraints, we define incremental deformation stages within which specific faults were active and particular loading conditions existed. A series of sequential elastic models were run and displacements were recorded at observation points that represent various stratigraphic surfaces. We compare synthetic stratigraphic surfaces with seis- mically interpreted stratigraphic surfaces represented on structure contour maps. Correlation between modeled and interpreted displacement fields indicates that iterative elastic models can provide insight into the stress fields at Elk Hills.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
